      "record": "78-83",
      "detail": "5th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The season has been stripped to its barest bones now - one last day, one last turn at Rogers Centre, and then the Blue Jays can finally stop pretending there's a race left to run. <strong>Saturday's 5-1 loss to the Reds</strong> dropped Toronto to 78-83, 5th in the AL East, and it also framed the day's real drama: Max Scherzer gets the ball in the finale, with retirement left hanging in the air like a late-autumn fog. Trey Yesavage's brief cameo to finish 2026 gave the club one more look at a young arm worth watching; that matters more now than the standings, which are dead and buried. The work ahead is plain enough - sort the pitching, find more bats, and decide which youngsters belong in the picture. <strong>Today's finale against Cincinnati is the last regular-season game, and then the winter work begins.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated September 27, 2026"
      },
